{"answer":"For x≠2, x²−4=(x−2)(x+2), so (x²−4)/(x−2)=x+2. The original quotient has domain ℝ\\{2}. Although x+2 is defined for every real x and equals 4 at x=2, the original quotient is undefined there. Thus the expressions agree exactly on ℝ\\{2}, but are not equivalent as functions at every real input.","approaches":[{"description":"Factor the numerator, retain the original denominator restriction, and cancel only on the restricted domain.","id":"factor-domain","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This proves the simplification while preserving the excluded input."},{"description":"Regard cancellation as defining the original quotient at every real input.","id":"global-cancel","outcome":"rejected","reason":"At x=2 the original denominator is zero, whereas x+2 has the value 4."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Direct expansion in factor-identity.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"factorization","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every real x, x²−4=(x−2)(x+2).","step_ids":["factor-identity"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Domain analysis in denominator-condition and original-domain-step.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"original-domain-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The domain of (x²−4)/(x−2) over the reals is ℝ\\{2}.","step_ids":["denominator-condition","original-domain-step"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Conditional cancellation in restricted-cancellation.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["factorization","original-domain-claim"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"restricted-simplification","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every real x in ℝ\\{2}, (x²−4)/(x−2)=x+2.","step_ids":["restricted-cancellation"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Comparison at the excluded input in compare-at-two.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["original-domain-claim","restricted-simplification"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"not-global-equivalence","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The expression x+2 is not equivalent to the original quotient at every real number; they agree exactly on ℝ\\{2}.","step_ids":["compare-at-two","agreement-domain"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"factor-identity","justification":"Expanding (x−2)(x+2) gives x²+2x−2x−4=x²−4.","statement":"Establish x²−4=(x−2)(x+2) for every real x."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"denominator-condition","justification":"A real quotient is defined only when its denominator is nonzero; x−2=0 exactly when x=2.","statement":"The original quotient is defined only when x≠2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["denominator-condition"],"id":"original-domain-step","justification":"For x≠2 the denominator is nonzero and the polynomial numerator is defined; for x=2 the denominator is zero.","statement":"The original quotient has domain ℝ\\{2}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["factor-identity","denominator-condition"],"id":"restricted-cancellation","justification":"When x≠2, the factor x−2 is nonzero, so ((x−2)(x+2))/(x−2)=x+2.","statement":"On ℝ\\{2}, simplify the quotient to x+2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["original-domain-step","restricted-cancellation"],"id":"compare-at-two","justification":"The polynomial x+2 is defined at every real number and gives 2+2=4, while the original quotient is undefined at x=2.","statement":"At x=2, x+2 has value 4 but the original quotient has no value."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["restricted-cancellation","compare-at-two"],"id":"agreement-domain","justification":"Restricted-cancellation gives equality for every x≠2, and compare-at-two shows that global equivalence fails at the excluded input.","statement":"The expressions agree exactly on ℝ\\{2}, not at every real input."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
